package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Five suspenseful, character-led serials with complete endings. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch13 {
 val stories = listOf(
 StorySeries("the_last_recipe_book","The Last Recipe Book","A family restaurant faces closure until a young cook discovers the story behind its famous dish.",listOf(
 StoryPart(1,"The Empty Table",listOf("Dara arrived at her grandmother's restaurant to find every table empty. A new supermarket had opened nearby, and customers had drifted away. Mama Nkem was studying a letter from the landlord: the restaurant had two months to prove it could pay the rent. Dara offered to help, though she knew little about running a business.","In the storeroom she found a cloth-wrapped recipe book filled with handwritten notes and drawings. Its first page said, 'A meal is never only what is in the pot.' The handwriting belonged to her great-grandfather, who had opened the restaurant decades earlier.")),
 StoryPart(2,"The Missing Ingredient",listOf("The famous pepper soup recipe had one line scratched out. Beside it, her great-grandfather had written, 'Ask the person who taught me to listen.' Dara expected a rare spice, but her grandmother said the soup had never depended on anything expensive.","An old photograph showed her great-grandfather beside a woman named Auntie Boma, Market Lane. Mama Nkem remembered that Boma had once sold herbs nearby. Dara copied the address and decided to visit with her grandmother.")),
 StoryPart(3,"Auntie Boma's Stall",listOf("Boma remembered the restaurant immediately. Dara's great-grandfather, she said, had been a good cook but an even better listener. He asked customers what reminded them of home, then adjusted a meal so each person felt welcome.","The scratched-out line contained no spice. It instructed the cook to ask what the diner needed: less heat, more broth, a smaller portion, or simply a quiet table. Dara began to understand why the old book was full of names.")),
 StoryPart(4,"The Names in the Margins",listOf("The notes described meals delivered after an illness, students allowed to pay later, and older customers who preferred a quiet corner. The restaurant had kept a record of people, not just orders.","Mama Nkem admitted she had stopped offering flexible meals because the accounts had become difficult. Dara proposed a small, budgeted community-meal programme with clear limits and transparent costs. Her grandmother agreed to test it for a week.")),
 StoryPart(5,"A Review That Hurt",listOf("The first week brought more customers, but an online review called the restaurant slow and old-fashioned. Dara wanted to reply angrily. Mama Nkem asked her to read the whole review: the kitchen had tried to prepare too many different orders at once.","They apologised and simplified the menu. Dara designed a clear board showing ingredients, prices, and waiting times. They invited honest feedback and began measuring waste. Criticism was uncomfortable, but it gave them something practical to improve.")),
 StoryPart(6,"The Recipe Test",listOf("Dara and Mama Nkem prepared the old pepper soup exactly as written. It tasted good, but not like the version people remembered. Boma noticed that the broth had been simmered too quickly; the old heavy pot held heat differently.","They tested small batches and recorded every change. Dara learned to measure, taste, and adjust instead of relying on vague instructions. Boma agreed to teach the kitchen team the old method and explain the story behind it.")),
 StoryPart(7,"The Dinner That Almost Failed",listOf("They announced a heritage dinner, then the refrigerator stopped working the night before. Mama Nkem considered cancelling. Dara called each guest, explained the problem, and offered a new time or a smaller menu.","A repairer confirmed that a replacement part was needed. They bought fresh ingredients from trusted vendors and reduced the menu rather than risk serving food that had not been stored safely. The event would be smaller, but the promise would be kept honestly.")),
 StoryPart(8,"Stories at the Table",listOf("The dinner began with fewer guests than expected, but every seat was filled. Boma explained how the recipe had changed over time. Mama Nkem read public notes from the old book only after checking that the families involved were comfortable with it.","A retired teacher recognised her father's name and described how the restaurant had helped her family when they first moved to the neighbourhood. Other guests shared memories. Dara realised the restaurant's strongest asset was a history people wanted to take part in.")),
 StoryPart(9,"The New Ledger",listOf("The takings improved, but Mama Nkem knew memories alone would not pay the rent. A small-business adviser helped create a realistic budget, reduce waste, and set a limit for sponsored meals. Dara organised a simple order sheet so the kitchen could work efficiently.","The landlord agreed to review the rent in three months rather than close the restaurant immediately. It was not a permanent guarantee, but it gave the family a fair chance. Dara added a new page to the book: 'A good tradition must learn how to continue.'")),
 StoryPart(10,"The Table Stays",listOf("Three months later, the restaurant was modest but busy at lunch and steady in the evenings. The restored pepper soup became a weekend special, and Boma visited every Friday to teach a kitchen skill. The old recipe book sat in a glass case, while a working copy stayed beside the stove.","Dara wrote: 'We kept the recipe by keeping the reason for it.' The restaurant had not been saved by a miracle ingredient. It survived because its people listened, adapted, kept honest accounts, and made room for the stories that brought customers through the door.")))),
 StorySeries("the_bridge_that_sang","The Bridge That Sang","A strange sound beneath an old bridge leads two friends to uncover a neglected engineering design.",listOf(
 StoryPart(1,"A Note at Dusk",listOf("Every evening, a low musical note drifted from the old railway bridge. Kelechi and Rina heard it on their way home from science club. Rina recorded the sound and noticed a pattern: one long note, two short ones, then silence.","The bridge was fenced off after a safety inspection. Their teacher warned them not to enter and suggested that the sound might come from air moving through a gap or a loose metal panel. The mystery was why it happened at exactly the same time.")),
 StoryPart(2,"The Old Timetable",listOf("A library timetable showed that a freight train once crossed at 6:15 each evening. A photograph showed a maintenance team beside metal pipes attached to the bridge supports.","Their teacher explained that the pipes might have carried warning tones along a maintenance walkway. The railway service had ended twenty years earlier. The students asked for a supervised inspection from the public path.")),
 StoryPart(3,"The Retired Inspector",listOf("A retired inspector, Mr Afolabi, confirmed that the pipes formed an old acoustic warning system. The bridge was closed after corrosion was found, and the system had long been disconnected.","He suspected that a drainage channel beneath the bridge resonated when a nearby pumping station started. He contacted the council engineer and requested an authorised inspection. The students agreed to observe only from the safe public area.")),
 StoryPart(4,"The Engineer's Visit",listOf("Engineer Ms Osei examined the external supports from an authorised platform. A loose protective cover vibrated when the pump started, producing the repeated note. She secured the area and arranged a maintenance inspection.","Mr Afolabi looked troubled when he saw the pipe arrangement. He explained that a young technician named Bisi had designed the original system, believing workers needed a warning they could hear even when the station bell failed.")),
 StoryPart(5,"The Unfinished Warning",listOf("Bisi's final report had never entered the archive. A public railway record mentioned a reference number but no document. The students searched donated local-history papers with help from the librarian.","They found an old notebook describing a drainage blockage and a maintenance schedule. Bisi had warned that the channel needed regular checks, but the report was filed under the wrong project code. The sound was not a secret message; it was a clue that the channel needed attention.")),
 StoryPart(6,"The Notebook's Lesson",listOf("Ms Osei thanked the students and arranged for the maintenance team to inspect the channel. The crew found a blockage and cleared it, then documented corrosion that still required treatment.","The notebook also revealed that Bisi's warning system had been tested successfully but abandoned when electric signals were introduced. Her work had not been useless; it had simply been forgotten when technology changed.")),
 StoryPart(7,"A Community Disagreement",listOf("A shop owner complained that the inspection would close the nearby footpath. Another resident wanted the bridge demolished. At a community meeting, the students heard both concerns and realised that safety, access, and local history all mattered.","Rina prepared a presentation about the sound and Kelechi mapped alternative walking routes using council information. They did not claim the bridge could reopen. Only qualified engineers could decide that. Their aim was to help residents understand the choices.")),
 StoryPart(8,"The Repair Plan",listOf("The council assessment found that the bridge needed major work but could potentially be preserved as a pedestrian crossing. The plan included drainage repairs, treatment of corroded sections, and a small display explaining Bisi's design.","The shop owner agreed to display the temporary route map. Residents petitioned for the bridge to be considered in the heritage plan. The students were invited to present their research, knowing that evidence could inform a decision but could not guarantee one.")),
 StoryPart(9,"The Last Evening Note",listOf("Before maintenance began, Ms Osei arranged a supervised demonstration from the safe public platform. At 6:15 the pump started and the pipe sounded once more. Everyone now understood the cause. The council recorded the sound for the heritage display before removing the faulty cover.","The crew cleared the drainage channel and completed its inspection. The bridge remained closed while repairs were planned, but the neighbourhood stopped treating it as a useless obstacle. It was shared history with a possible future, provided the work was done carefully.")),
 StoryPart(10,"A Crossing for Tomorrow",listOf("A year later, engineers completed the required repairs and safety checks, and the bridge reopened as a pedestrian route. A plaque explained Bisi's warning system and credited the workers who had maintained the crossing.","Kelechi and Rina crossed at sunset, hearing only footsteps. They had listened, checked records, and involved experts instead of trespassing or guessing. The bridge no longer sang, but its story had finally found a voice.")))),
 StorySeries("the_day_the_library_moved","The Day the Library Moved","A neighbourhood races to preserve its library service when the building must close for repairs.",listOf(
 StoryPart(1,"Boxes in the Reading Room",listOf("A notice announced that the neighbourhood library would close in six weeks because its roof needed major repairs. The books would move to a central branch twelve kilometres away. For children, older residents, and people without easy transport, that meant losing their nearest place to read.","Favour had spent every Wednesday there since learning to read. Librarian Mr James explained that the building could not stay open during repairs, but a temporary reading room might be possible. Favour gathered friends and began listing places that could host one.")),
 StoryPart(2,"The Empty Shop",listOf("An empty shop near the bus stop seemed perfect, but its owner worried about security, electricity, and rent. The students measured the room with an adult volunteer and drew a layout with clear walkways and shelves.","Mr James explained that books needed a catalogue, dry storage, and a reliable borrowing system. He offered to create a smaller travelling collection while the council reviewed the building. They needed more than a room; they needed a service people could trust.")),
 StoryPart(3,"The Lost Catalogue",listOf("The transfer revealed that the local-history catalogue was incomplete. Donated books had been recorded in an old ledger but never entered into the computer system. The missing list included oral histories, photographs, and handwritten accounts from early residents.","The volunteers compared the ledger with shelves under supervision. They found misfiled books and one missing collection of market stories. Its donor, Mrs Ekanem, lived above the old tailor shop. Favour decided to ask whether she remembered it.")),
 StoryPart(4,"The Donor's Condition",listOf("Mrs Ekanem remembered lending the book to a former librarian who wanted to make copies. She was happy to donate it again, but asked that private family accounts not be shared online without permission.","Mr James agreed. The library would preserve the book, catalogue it, and mark sections that could be shared publicly. Favour helped write a permission note for future volunteers. Saving a story did not mean making every detail public.")),
 StoryPart(5,"A Room Above the Clinic",listOf("The empty shop proved too expensive, but the clinic manager offered a meeting room two afternoons a week. It was accessible by ramp and close to the bus stop. The room could not hold the whole collection, so they planned rotating books and reading sessions.","The clinic needed the room on other days, and library activities had to remain separate from clinical services. They agreed on a schedule, a lockable cabinet, and an adult supervisor. The temporary library would be smaller, but it could open before the old building closed.")),
 StoryPart(6,"The First Reading Day",listOf("Only seven visitors arrived on the first day. Favour had expected a crowd and felt disappointed. Then an older man asked for a large-print edition of a novel his late wife used to borrow.","Mr James arranged a transfer from the central branch. The man returned with neighbours, one carrying a puzzle book and another offering to read aloud. The room began to feel like a library because people knew they could ask for help.")),
 StoryPart(7,"The Book That Came Back",listOf("A package arrived with no return address. Inside was the missing market-stories book and a note apologising for keeping it too long. The sender had found it while clearing a relative's house and wanted it returned to the community.","Mrs Ekanem identified her handwriting in the margins. She agreed to record an introduction but chose not to discuss private accounts. The library catalogued the book with clear access notes and its history of donation.")),
 StoryPart(8,"The Roof Gives Way",listOf("Heavy rain caused a leak before the planned closure. Staff moved the remaining books sooner than expected. Volunteers helped only with approved light tasks while trained staff handled the collection and building safety. Favour feared the temporary room would not be ready.","The clinic offered an extra afternoon, a school lent tables, and a printer donated signs. The group updated opening hours on noticeboards so nobody would make a wasted journey. The service continued even though the familiar building had closed.")),
 StoryPart(9,"More Than Shelves",listOf("The temporary library developed homework help, a reading circle, large-print requests, and local-history afternoons. The central branch supplied requested titles. Favour learned to record borrowing carefully and ask before photographing activities.","Residents presented evidence of use at a council meeting and requested a repair timetable and accessible entrance. The council published a schedule and appointed a community representative to provide updates. The closure now had a public plan.")),
 StoryPart(10,"The Library Comes Home",listOf("Months later, the library reopened with a stronger roof, better lighting, and an accessible entrance. The temporary room closed, though the clinic kept a small book exchange. The local-history collection returned with its catalogue corrected and privacy rules clearly marked.","Favour placed the market-stories book beside a new notebook for reader suggestions. Mr James reminded everyone that the library was more than a building: it was a promise that knowledge should be reachable. The first visitors arrived carrying books borrowed from the temporary room, ready for another chapter.")))),
 StorySeries("the_clockwork_orchestra","The Clockwork Orchestra","Students restore an abandoned mechanical instrument and uncover the disagreement that silenced it.",listOf(
 StoryPart(1,"The Music Behind the Wall",listOf("During rehearsal, a rhythm tapped from behind the auditorium wall: three taps, a pause, then five. The caretaker found a wooden cabinet with brass gears behind a storage panel. A label called it the Harmony Engine, built by a former science teacher.","The cabinet was too heavy and delicate to move. Music teacher Mr Eze told the students not to touch it and asked the school archivist to search the records. Nobody knew whether the instrument had ever worked.")),
 StoryPart(2,"The Teacher's Signature",listOf("The archive identified the inventor as Mrs Amina Yusuf, who taught music and mechanics forty years earlier. Her notes described rotating cylinders that played patterns on tuned metal bars, helping students understand rhythm through sound and movement.","The final entry said the instrument had stopped during a performance. Mrs Yusuf requested that it remain untouched until a missing component was found. The students wondered whether that component was still somewhere at school.")),
 StoryPart(3,"The Missing Cylinder",listOf("A diagram showed six cylinders, but the cabinet held five. The missing one was labelled 'welcome.' An old concert programme used the same word and listed a visiting school orchestra whose joint performance had been cancelled.","The retired music coordinator remembered that a student had taken the cylinder for safekeeping after a disagreement about the concert order. She could not recall where it went, but she remembered the student's name: Kola.")),
 StoryPart(4,"A Box of Programmes",listOf("A box of programmes contained Kola's letter. He had helped Mrs Yusuf build the cylinders. The visiting orchestra had been promised the final performance, but the principal changed the order and gave the slot to a sponsor's guest.","Kola removed the welcome cylinder because he feared the school would use it without crediting the students who built the instrument. Mr Eze suggested hearing Kola's account before judging a decision made decades earlier.")),
 StoryPart(5,"Finding Kola",listOf("The alumni office located Kola, now a retired instrument technician. He remembered the machine and agreed to meet the students. He had wanted the school to acknowledge the student makers and give them a proper place in the concert.","Kola still had the cylinder in a labelled case. It was intact, but its paper pattern had faded. He offered to lend it if a qualified conservator checked the mechanism and the new programme credited Mrs Yusuf and the original student builders.")),
 StoryPart(6,"A Careful Restoration",listOf("The school contacted a museum instrument conservator. She inspected the cabinet and explained which parts could be cleaned and which should remain untouched. The students learned that restoration meant preserving original material, not making everything look new.","The conservator found a loosened spring and repaired it using an approved process. At low speed, the brass bars produced a faint sequence of notes. The cylinder could be copied for practice while the original was stored safely.")),
 StoryPart(7,"The Argument Returns",listOf("An alumnus complained that the old concert dispute was being presented unfairly. He said the sponsor had paid for auditorium repairs and had asked only for a short welcome, not control of the performance. The former principal confirmed that the schedule had changed but said he never intended to erase the students' work.","The students realised the story had more than one side. They decided the new concert should acknowledge the disagreement honestly without turning it into a contest over blame. The school also committed to crediting every maker.")),
 StoryPart(8,"A New Arrangement",listOf("The group wrote a piece using the five surviving rhythms and a copied welcome cylinder. Each section represented the builders, performers, teachers, visitors, and people who maintained the instrument. Everyone received a moment in the arrangement.","Kola suggested a slower opening so the gears could settle. The conservator approved the test procedure, and the students rehearsed with a modern keyboard as backup. They wanted the performance to succeed without forcing the antique machine beyond safe limits.")),
 StoryPart(9,"Concert Night",listOf("The Harmony Engine began with a gentle pattern, joined by choir and ensemble. When the welcome rhythm arrived, the students named Mrs Yusuf, Kola, and the original student makers. The audience heard the old instrument play in public for the first time in decades.","The machine paused before the final section. Mr Eze signalled the backup ensemble, and the music continued without anyone rushing to restart the mechanism. The audience applauded both the instrument and the students' calm response.")),
 StoryPart(10,"The Sound We Keep",listOf("The Harmony Engine became the centrepiece of a school exhibition. The original cylinder was stored safely, while a working copy was used for demonstrations. The archive displayed the concert letters and restoration notes, including the disagreement and what the school had learned about credit.","The students recorded a new piece inspired by the machine. It did not need to play every day to matter. Its sound had brought old makers and new musicians into the same room, and the school had learned to honour the people behind the music.")))),
 StorySeries("the_green_line_project","The Green Line Project","Young neighbours turn an overlooked strip of land into a safer route and a living corridor.",listOf(
 StoryPart(1,"The Shortcut",listOf("The strip behind the bus depot was barely wide enough for two people. Broken paving and poor lighting made it difficult after sunset. When a younger pupil slipped there in the rainy season, Ifeoma decided the neighbourhood needed a safer path.","Her friends doubted the council would listen. Ifeoma used the public reporting form and gathered observations from people who used the route. She recorded locations and conditions without photographing anyone without permission.")),
 StoryPart(2,"The Old Survey",listOf("A retired surveyor showed Ifeoma an old map. The strip had once been a planted boundary, with a drainage channel beneath it and a second entrance now blocked by a fence.","The surveyor warned that the route could not simply be widened. The underground drain had to remain accessible, and the depot needed a clear safety boundary. Ifeoma asked the council for an authorised site visit.")),
 StoryPart(3,"The Depot Manager",listOf("The depot manager worried that a project might interfere with deliveries. He explained that vehicles needed a turning area and the fence marked a safety zone. The students asked whether the path could be improved without entering operational space.","A council planner joined a site walk. They identified a narrow route outside the vehicle boundary, but it needed drainage, lighting, and separation from the depot gate. It was possible only with technical approval and a maintenance agreement.")),
 StoryPart(4,"The Gardeners' Advice",listOf("A local gardening group recommended hardy plants that would not block sightlines. Some residents wanted a tall hedge, but the depot manager explained that visibility near the gate was essential.","The gardeners proposed low planting beds, a clear walkway, and small trees only where underground services allowed. They offered to teach volunteers how to care for the plants. Ifeoma put safety before appearance in her revised plan.")),
 StoryPart(5,"A Budget with No Guesswork",listOf("The council requested a cost estimate. The students first priced only paving stones and plants. A contractor helped them include drainage, lighting, accessible gradients, signs, waste removal, and maintenance. The total was much larger than expected.","Ifeoma divided the project into stages. The first would repair the most dangerous section and improve lighting; planting could follow. A community grant might cover materials, while the council would need to approve the technical work.")),
 StoryPart(6,"The Petition",listOf("The petition gathered signatures from residents, shopkeepers, bus drivers, and parents. Some opposed the project because they feared more foot traffic near the depot. Ifeoma invited them to explain their concerns rather than dismiss them.","They agreed that the path should close during depot emergencies, include clear signs, and have no seating near the vehicle entrance. The students revised the proposal and recorded concerns alongside benefits.")),
 StoryPart(7,"The Storm Test",listOf("A heavy storm flooded the strip before the council meeting. The students observed from the safe public pavement and reported the blocked drain. The maintenance team found leaves and rubbish around an old grate.","The storm proved that paving alone would not solve the problem. The planner added a drainage inspection point and a maintenance schedule. Ifeoma included the observations so the plan reflected real conditions.")),
 StoryPart(8,"A Vote at the Hall",listOf("At the community hall, the depot manager explained safety boundaries, the planner presented the design, and gardeners described the planting plan. Ifeoma admitted that the first version had overlooked maintenance costs and thanked residents who challenged it.","The committee approved the staged proposal and agreed to apply for funding. The depot offered staff time to maintain the boundary fence, while residents volunteered for planting days. Formal council checks were still required.")),
 StoryPart(9,"The First Green Strip",listOf("Professional crews completed the drainage and paving work. Students stayed behind safety barriers and helped with approved tasks such as plant labels and information boards. New lights and a clear closure sign were installed.","After inspection, the route reopened. It was not yet a garden corridor, but it was safer. A month later, gardeners led a planting day, and neighbours who had once disagreed worked side by side.")),
 StoryPart(10,"A Route That Belongs",listOf("By year's end, the Green Line was a recognised walking route. Planting beds were growing, drainage checks were on a public schedule, and the depot manager had a direct contact for reporting damage. The council included it in its neighbourhood improvement report.","Ifeoma kept the first rough map with its crossed-out ideas and corrected measurements. It showed how the project had improved through listening. The green line was proof that careful cooperation could turn a complaint into something useful for everyone."))))
 )
}
